# Cross-compile on the builder's native platform: xcaddy honours GOOS/GOARCH,
# so `--platform linux/amd64,linux/arm64` works from one Dockerfile. The only
# step that runs inside the target image is the final stage's `apk upgrade`,
# which needs QEMU for arm64 (release-docker.yml sets it up).
#
# Security pins:
#   - The FLOOR --with lines (crowdsec/pkg/apiclient, x/net/http2, chi/v5,
#     go-jose/v4, klauspost/compress -- currently the first FIVE) are version
#     floors. Keep every floor above the plugin lines, and update this list
#     when one is added. xcaddy runs `go get` once
#     per --with, in order, and `go get pkg@ver` sets exactly that version, so
#     they must come first: a plugin fetched after them can still raise these
#     modules but never lowers them.
#     crowdsec/pkg/apiclient @v1.7.8 is the documented CVE-BEARING minimum
#     (past CVE-2026-44982 and CVE-2026-44981) but is NOT a clean version and
#     is NOT what ships: two CVE-less advisories reach <= 1.7.8 (GHSA-rh69,
#     GHSA-g2x2, both patched 1.8.0) -- see the crowdsec block below, which
#     measures why neither is linked. What protects the image is that MVS
#     resolves higher, not this number: caddy-crowdsec-bouncer @v0.15.0 below
#     requires crowdsec v1.8.1, so MVS resolves to 1.8.1 and this floor is
#     currently slack. It stays as the stated minimum, so a future bouncer
#     wanting an older crowdsec cannot silently drop below it. Check the built
#     binary, not this line, for the version in the image.
#     x/net/http2 @v0.62.0 lifts golang.org/x/net past CVE-2026-78669,
#     CVE-2026-78661 and CVE-2026-97045.
#     chi/v5 @v5.5.0 lifts go-chi past CVE-2026-114437 and CVE-2026-118681
#     (auth bypass in chi middleware.SupressNotFound). It does NOT clear
#     CVE-2026-122132 -- and the reason that is easy to miss is the subject of
#     the chi block below, which is where a reader checking this pin should go.
#     go-jose/v4 @v4.1.7 lifts go-jose past SIX live advisories, not one:
#     CVE-2026-116587 (HIGH, = GHSA-mgcc-cww5-fp2q, fixed in 4.1.6) PLUS five
#     published 2026-10-30 with NO CVE id -- v6h2, c67m, p9j4, r39w, 27pc --
#     each vulnerable at <= v4.1.6 and patched only in v4.1.7. So 4.1.7 is
#     REQUIRED, not currency; 4.1.6 would be insufficient. Those five are
#     absent from OSV and from GitHub's global advisory index (both 404) and
#     are visible only via repos/go-jose/go-jose/security-advisories, because
#     an advisory with no CVE id is not promoted into the global or ecosystem
#     indexes -- the same blind spot documented for crowdsec below. Do not
#     conclude "no advisories" for any pin here from OSV alone (T-0646).
#     go-jose/v4 was ALREADY in this image transitively at v4.1.5, so this pin
#     is a version floor on an existing module, not a new dependency. A
#     production import path exists via smallstep/certificates ->
#     coreos/go-oidc/v3 -> go-jose/v4 (oidc/jose.go, jwks.go, verify.go),
#     reachable only with `acme_server` configured, which no fleet host does.
#     The sibling go-jose/v3 line (v3.0.5) is untouched and still carries
#     advisories with no v3 fix published at all -- see T-0645.
#     Caddy already links these packages, so the blank import xcaddy adds for
#     each changes nothing else. The build fails if `go get` ever reports a
#     downgrade, so a pin can't quietly pull a plugin back to an older release.
#   - crowdsec @v1.7.8 carries THREE GitHub advisories that OSV does not hold at
#     all -- not by package, not by the pinned version's commit, and not by id
#     (`GET https://api.osv.dev/v1/vulns/<ghsa>` returns `code 5` for each).
#     None has a CVE id, the same pattern T-0646 found for go-jose, so Trivy and
#     the image gate are blind to them too (T-0628) and a green gate is not
#     evidence here. All three are present-but-NOT-LINKED, measured on the
#     published binary and on the module graph rather than assumed (T-0653):
#       GHSA-rh69-4vqj-9gj8  medium  <= 1.7.8   fixed 1.8.0  kubernetes-audit
#                            acquisition webhook, unbounded request-body read
#       GHSA-g2x2-jgfg-pg7g  medium  <= 1.7.8   fixed 1.8.0  HTTP acquisition
#                            datasource, no decompressed-body cap, trusts
#                            Content-Length
#       GHSA-g9hc-g4w6-4mfx  low     <= 1.8.1   NO FIX YET   hardcoded
#                            InsecureSkipVerify in the notification-splunk
#                            plugin (cmd/notification-splunk/main.go:116)
#     This image pins `crowdsec/pkg/apiclient` -- the LAPI *client*, which is
#     what caddy-crowdsec-bouncer talks to. The whole transitive package
#     closure of that pin plus the bouncer is six packages: pkg/apiclient,
#     pkg/apiclient/useragent, pkg/fflag, pkg/models, pkg/modelscapi and
#     pkg/types. `go mod why` answers "main module does not need package" for
#     pkg/acquisition, both affected acquisition modules AND pkg/csplugin, and
#     `strings` on the binary from :stable finds 0 refs to any of them (against
#     456 for pkg/models as a control). The two acquisition bugs are how a
#     crowdsec AGENT ingests logs; this image runs no agent. The splunk one is
#     `package main` in cmd/, a standalone plugin binary Go cannot link into
#     another program even in principle -- so its missing fix cannot bind here.
#     Therefore do NOT raise this floor for these three: v1.8.0 would clear two
#     of them and v1.8.2 still would not clear the splunk one, while buying no
#     reachable fix and risking the build for a bouncer that needs 1.7.x.
#     A FOURTH row, GHSA-273h-gvwr-c3qj (CVE-2026-44981, LAPI unbounded gzip
#     decompression), is already cleared: its range is >= 1.7.0 patched in
#     1.7.8, i.e. exactly this pin. It still shows in the weekly report because
#     the range filter only drops a row on a demonstrable upper bound and that
#     range leads with `>=`. Conservative by design, not a bug.
#     Re-check if the bouncer ever links acquisition or notification code, or
#     if the splunk advisory gets a fixed release (T-0661).
#   - chi/v5 @v5.5.0 carries SEVEN GitHub advisories on the go-chi/chi repo.
#     FOUR survive this pin's range filter and recur in the weekly report as
#     "GitHub-only" rows; they are listed here so a reader who checks this pin
#     finds the answer instead of re-deriving it every week. NONE is linked.
#     Measured, not assumed (T-0652, re-measured T-0655, PoC re-run 2026-11-12
#     against v5.5.0, report re-run same day):
#       GHSA-mqqf-5wvp-8fh8  medium  >=5.2.2      NO FIX   open redirect in
#                            middleware.RedirectSlashes -- see the FALSE FIX
#                            note below, which is the important part
#       GHSA-3fxj-6jh8-hvhx  medium  >= 5.2.1     NO FIX   IP spoofing in
#                            middleware.RealIP
#       GHSA-9g5q-2w5x-hmxf  medium  since 0.9.0  NO FIX   IP spoofing via
#                            X-Forwarded-For in Request.RemoteAddr
#       GHSA-vrw8-fxc6-2r93  medium  v5.2.1       -        host-header
#                            injection -> open redirect in RedirectSlashes.
#                            Bounded below this pin, but the range is a BARE
#                            VERSION with no comparator, which the textual
#                            filter cannot prove excludes 5.5.0, so it is
#                            reported. Conservative by design, as with the
#                            crowdsec `>=` row above.
#     Two more are correctly filtered out and should NOT be expected in the
#     report: GHSA-rqrp-8cg2-5958 (<=5.4.0, = CVE-2026-118681, SupressNotFound
#     auth bypass) is genuinely CLEARED by this pin -- it is the advisory
#     v5.5.0 was taken for -- and GHSA-rjr7-jggh-pgcp (<=5.2.1) and
#     GHSA-j6qf-3m9c-vwj3 (<= 5.3.2) are bounded below it.
#     EVERY ONE of these lives in go-chi/chi/v5/middleware. That package is
#     NOT linked into this image: chi's core router is (105 string refs in the
#     v2.68.11 candidate binary, against 4250 for caddyserver/caddy/v2 as a
#     control) because Caddy uses it in caddypki/acmeserver and
#     smallstep/certificates imports it in acme/api, api, scep/api and
#     authority/admin/api -- but the middleware package has ZERO refs and the
#     symbols RedirectSlashes and SupressNotFound do not appear at all. The
#     binary is stripped, so `go tool nm` reports no symbols for anything
#     including Caddy itself; these counts come from `strings` with
#     known-present modules as controls, because the nm reading looks like a
#     clean answer while measuring nothing. So no middleware advisory is
#     reachable here and this floor is prophylactic -- worth keeping, because
#     smallstep/certificates ca/ca.go already imports chi/v5/middleware (for
#     GetHead only, never SupressNotFound) and that `ca` package is one import
#     away from being linked; the floor makes such a build take a fixed chi.
#     Note this is the IMAGE. The CaddyUI server binary is a separate program
#     that DOES import chi/v5/middleware (internal/server/server.go), but it
#     mounts only RequestLogger and Recoverer -- neither RedirectSlashes nor
#     SupressNotFound appears anywhere in this repo's Go source. Re-check that
#     if either is ever mounted.
#     *** THE CHI GATE IS A KNOWN-FALSE GREEN -- do not read it as clean. ***
#     GHSA-mqqf-5wvp-8fh8 covers TWO CVEs under one id: `gh api
#     /advisories/GHSA-mqqf-5wvp-8fh8` gives cve_id CVE-2025-69725 with
#     `>= 5.2.2, < 5.2.4`. v5.2.4 fixed the BACKSLASH variant; CVE-2026-122132
#     is the TAB bypass OF that fix, and GitHub kept only the first boundary.
#     OSV mirrors that truncated range (`introduced 5.2.2, fixed 5.2.4`), so
#     WHICH OSV QUESTION YOU ASK DECIDES THE ANSWER -- measured 2026-11-12:
#       package+version  chi/v5 @5.5.0            -> 0 vulns   (FALSE CLEAN)
#       by the tag's commit 3a33ba578e0d          -> CVE-2026-122132
#     The package+version query is the one an SBOM scanner asks, so Trivy and
#     caddy-image-gate.sh report chi clean. check-pinned-modules.sh asks BOTH
#     -- that second, commit-scoped query is exactly why this pin still shows
#     an advisory in the weekly report when Trivy shows none, and the report is
#     the one that is right. Do not "reconcile" them by dropping the commit
#     query. The standalone CVE-2026-122132 OSV record (package field null,
#     GIT range, 9 affected versions) and NVD both still list 5.2.2 through
#     5.5.0 affected, and the TAB bypass is live on the newest tag: running
#     scripts/poc/redirectslashes-tab.go (usage in its header) against v5.5.0 returns
#     `Location: "/\t/evil.com"` for a request to `/%09/evil.com/` (301).
#     RedirectSlashes is byte-identical between v5.2.4 and v5.5.0. A browser
#     strips the TAB, resolves //evil.com and follows it off-site.
#     This is strictly stronger than the T-0628 / T-0646 "OSV is blind to
#     CVE-less advisories" caveat: there the index held nothing, here it holds
#     a positive clean answer that is false. Settle it with the PoC, not with a
#     version query. There is no fixed tag to bump to; go-chi/chi#1238 is open.
#     Re-check when a tag lands that makes that PoC print no raw TAB (T-0664).
#   - caddy-crowdsec-bouncer is back on a tag. It was pinned to raw commit
#     bdd7e516 because it was the only upstream code supporting CrowdSec 1.7
#     (v0.14.1, the newest tag then, needed 1.6). v0.15.0 (2026-10-30) requires
#     crowdsec v1.8.1, so that reason is gone and an ordinary version comparison
#     works again. This tag is also upstream main's HEAD, so the pin is current
#     as well as legible. It is what actually sets the crowdsec version (1.8.1),
#     above the floor near the top of this block.
#   - EVERY --with below carries a version. An unpinned --with re-resolves to
#     whatever is latest at build time, so a base-layer refresh (an Alpine CVE,
#     a -pN tag) could pull new third-party code into the binary on all six
#     boxes — including a DNS provider holding TLS-cert API credentials and the
#     Coraza WAF. caddy-image-gate.sh blocks such a build from publishing by
#     diffing `caddy list-modules --versions`, but that is detection after the
#     fact: it stops the refresh rather than the drift, so an unrelated plugin
#     release holds the CVE fix hostage until a human investigates. Pinning
#     makes a plugin bump a deliberate edit, reviewed and versioned like any
#     other change, and leaves the gate as a second, independent check.
#     The trade, accepted knowingly: plugin fixes no longer arrive on their own
#     and need a deliberate bump here — the same trade already made for
#     crowdsec, x/net and chi.
#     caddy-dns/digitalocean has never been tagged upstream, so its pin is the
#     pseudo-version of master@04bde286 (2025-06-06) — the exact commit the
#     published image already contains, not a new choice.
#   - klauspost/compress @v1.20.4 lifts the module past GO-2026-6865 /
#     CVE-2026-116591 / GHSA-fpmh-frq8-7rj8 (HIGH, CWE-190): a crafted ~27 KB
#     two-block zstd frame panics DecodeAll and the streaming decoder with an
#     out-of-range slice index instead of returning a decode error. Vulnerable
#     <= v1.20.1, patched v1.20.2. The published image shipped v1.20.1.
#     THIS PIN IS LOAD-BEARING, NOT SLACK. MVS resolves to exactly v1.20.1 on
#     its own -- caddy 2.11.9, caddy-l4 v0.1.3, caddy-crowdsec-bouncer v0.15.0
#     and prometheus/client_golang v1.25.0 each require v1.20.1 and nothing in
#     the graph requires higher -- so without this line the build stays on the
#     vulnerable version. Unlike the crowdsec floor above, removing it
#     regresses the image. The module ROOT carries a Go package
#     (compressible.go), so a plain `--with <module>@ver` links and no
#     `--replace` is needed.
#     TRIVY IS BLIND TO THIS ONE, and NOT for the CVE-less reason seen with
#     go-jose and crowdsec -- this advisory HAS a CVE id and IS in OSV. The
#     cause is a package-vs-module NAMING MISMATCH: OSV/GHSA name the affected
#     package `github.com/klauspost/compress/zstd` (the subpackage), while Go
#     binaries and go.mod record the MODULE `github.com/klauspost/compress`.
#     Trivy matches the recorded name, finds no advisory under the module path
#     and reports nothing -- while still listing the package at v1.20.1 in its
#     own Packages output. Measured: a probe go.mod requiring the module gives
#     0 findings and one requiring the zstd subpackage path gives the HIGH,
#     same scanner and same DB. govulncheck is unaffected because the Go
#     advisory names the module correctly and matches on symbols. So a THIRD
#     distinct mechanism can hide a finding from Trivy, after CVE-less
#     advisories (T-0646) and `--replace` invisibility (T-0710): a scanner's
#     silence is not evidence (T-0729).
#     Reachability, re-derived against the ARTIFACT rather than a hand-built
#     probe -- and that distinction mattered here. The build runs with
#     `-tags nobadger,nomysql,nopgx` (recorded in the binary's build info), so
#     dgraph-io/badger is NOT LINKED AT ALL: 0 strings for `dgraph-io/badger`
#     in the shipped binary. A first probe that omitted those tags and most
#     --with plugins produced a confident but WRONG badger/smallstep-nosql
#     route; a probe rebuilt with all 13 plugins, `modules/standard` and the
#     real tags reproduces the binary's klauspost package set exactly. Use
#     `go mod why -vendor` as well: WITHOUT -vendor it reports a prometheus
#     promhttp route that runs through `prometheus/client_golang/
#     prometheus.test` and is TEST-ONLY.
#     The real non-test importers are Caddy's OWN
#     modules/caddyhttp/encode/zstd and DeRuina/timberjack (log rotation) --
#     so this IS the HTTP compression stack.
#     IT IS STILL NOT REACHABLE WITH HOSTILE INPUT, for a sharper reason:
#     GO-2026-6865 is a DECODER bug, and Caddy's encode/zstd is ENCODER-ONLY.
#     zstd.go calls zstd.NewWriter and nothing else; zstd_precompressed.go
#     merely declares the `.zst` suffix so the file server can serve
#     pre-compressed files as-is. Caddy compresses RESPONSES; it never
#     decompresses a zstd REQUEST body. A sweep of the whole 1274-package
#     shipped closure for non-test `zstd.NewReader` / `.DecodeAll(` finds hits
#     only inside klauspost/compress/zstd itself, with a NEGATIVE CONTROL
#     confirming the same sweep DOES fire for zstd.NewWriter (on encode/zstd
#     and timberjack) -- so the silence is a measurement, not a broken scan.
#     govulncheck names fse.Compress/Decompress and huff0.Compress1X/
#     Compress4X/ReadTable/EstimateSizes/AppendTable as reachable because they
#     are present and linked; the *Compress* ones are genuinely live via the
#     encoder. So `reachable` here means LINKED, not EXPLOITABLE.
#     Bumped anyway: the fix is free, the pin is cheap, and any future config
#     or plugin that decodes zstd would make it live.
#     v1.20.4 (not the minimum v1.20.2) because v1.20.2/.3/.4 are all clean at
#     both the module and zstd-subpackage paths in OSV, repo-level GitHub
#     advisories show only two ever (both fixed at or below v1.20.2), and
#     v1.20.4 is the current release.
#     THE ASSERTION BELOW IS DELIBERATELY NARROW. It accepts only v1.20.x for
#     x >= 4, and the trailing [[:space:]] matters: without it a PRE-RELEASE
#     pseudo-version such as v1.20.4-0.2026... would match while sorting BELOW
#     v1.20.4. So a future legitimate bump to v1.21.0 or beyond FAILS THE
#     BUILD until this pattern is widened. That is intended -- it fails closed,
#     and re-taking the decision is the point -- but it is a build break to
#     expect, not a mystery. Widen the pattern in the same commit as the bump.
#   - A pin that is behind upstream ON PURPOSE carries a `# pin-watch: floor`
#     comment immediately above it, read by scripts/check-pinned-modules.sh.
#     Such a pin is reported under "Intentional floors" with its reason rather
#     than counted as behind, so a decision that has already been taken stops
#     producing an `attention` verdict on every weekly run — a signal that is
#     always on carries no information (T-0651). It is an ACKNOWLEDGEMENT, not
#     an exclusion: `pin=`, `against=` and `reason=` are all required, the
#     acknowledgement expires by itself once upstream moves past `against=`,
#     an advisory on the pinned version always outranks it, and a directive
#     whose `pin=` no longer matches the line below it is a hard error rather
#     than a floor quietly inherited by a new version. So re-pinning a floored
#     module means re-taking the decision, which is the point.
FROM --platform=$BUILDPLATFORM caddy:2.11.9-builder AS builder
ARG TARGETOS=linux
ARG TARGETARCH
RUN set -o pipefail && GOOS=${TARGETOS} GOARCH=${TARGETARCH} xcaddy build \
    # pin-watch: floor pin=v1.7.8 against=v1.8.2 ref=T-0636 reason=v1.7.8 is the exact version fixing CVE-2026-44982 and CVE-2026-44981, so it is a true CVE-BEARING boundary -- but NOT a clean version: 2 of the 3 GitHub-only advisories reach <= 1.7.8, and OSV is blind to all 3 because none carries a CVE id, so an OSV-clean reading of either version proves nothing (T-0646). All 3 are acquisition/notification code, outside the 6-package closure of pkg/apiclient + the bouncer, so none is linked and a bump buys no reachable fix; v1.8.2 still would not clear the splunk one. This floor is also currently SLACK: the bouncer at v0.15.0 requires crowdsec v1.8.1, so MVS resolves to 1.8.1 and the built binary is past the two acquisition advisories regardless. Kept so a future bouncer cannot silently drop below the CVE boundary. Measured: header, T-0653.
    --with github.com/crowdsecurity/crowdsec/pkg/apiclient@v1.7.8 \
    --with golang.org/x/net/http2@v0.62.0 \
    --with github.com/go-chi/chi/v5@v5.5.0 \
    --with github.com/go-jose/go-jose/v4@v4.1.7 \
    --with github.com/klauspost/compress@v1.20.4 \
    --with github.com/caddy-dns/cloudflare@v0.2.4 \
    --with github.com/caddy-dns/porkbun@v0.3.1 \
    --with github.com/caddy-dns/namecheap@v1.0.0 \
    --with github.com/caddy-dns/godaddy@v1.2.0 \
    --with github.com/caddy-dns/digitalocean@v0.0.0-20250606074528-04bde2867106 \
    --with github.com/caddy-dns/hetzner/v2@v2.0.1 \
    --with github.com/caddy-dns/route53@v1.6.2 \
    --with github.com/caddy-dns/gandi@v1.2.1 \
    --with github.com/hslatman/caddy-crowdsec-bouncer/http@v0.15.0 \
    --with github.com/mholt/caddy-ratelimit@v0.1.0 \
    --with github.com/mholt/caddy-l4@v0.1.3 \
    --with github.com/corazawaf/coraza-caddy/v2@v2.6.2 \
    2>&1 | tee /tmp/xcaddy.log \
    && if grep 'go: downgraded' /tmp/xcaddy.log; then echo 'a pin downgraded a module' >&2; exit 1; fi \
    && go version -m /usr/bin/caddy > /tmp/mods.txt \
    && if ! grep -qE '^[[:space:]]*dep[[:space:]]+github\.com/klauspost/compress[[:space:]]+v1\.20\.(4|[5-9]|[1-9][0-9])[[:space:]]' /tmp/mods.txt; then \
         echo 'klauspost/compress is not >= v1.20.4 in the built binary (GO-2026-6865)' >&2; \
         grep -E 'klauspost/compress' /tmp/mods.txt >&2 || true; exit 1; \
       fi

FROM caddy:2.11.9-alpine
# The caddy base image only gets Alpine fixes when it is rebuilt upstream;
# upgrade here so this image ships them (e.g. brotli-libs 1.2.1, CVE-2026-111497).
RUN apk upgrade --no-cache
COPY --from=builder /usr/bin/caddy /usr/bin/caddy
